{
  "title": "UI verify 2b: the verify prompt's Browser section and deterministic browser verdicts",
  "body": "## Problem\nPart of #33, part 2, slice b. Main now includes the confined Playwright MCP browser from #906/#890, and verify gets it when a preview is running. But the verify prompt doesn't tell the verifier to use it, and browser checks can still end environmentally `blocked` even though the browser was usable.\n\nThis slice makes the browser count. Keeping screenshots as local evidence is #1008, which is split off because it is a privacy boundary of its own.\n\n## Requirements\n1. **Identify browser checks before verify.** Add an additive, defaulted browser marker to acceptance criteria and holdout scenarios. Spec and holdout authors mark visual or interactive checks. The engine uses these persisted markers; the verifier cannot change their scope. Older records without markers retain legacy behavior.\n\n2. **Browser prompt.** When a preview is running, include its engine-provided URL and instruct the verifier to use the available browser at **1280 × 800** and **375 × 812 CSS pixels**. For every applicable browser check, request observations at both sizes, including actions and observed results for interactions. The verifier may take screenshots for its own inspection; this slice doesn't retain them (that is #1008). A screenshot alone does not establish interactive correctness.\n\n3. **Deterministic verdict handling.** For a marked browser check, when the engine’s same-attempt records show both a ready preview and successful browser setup, convert model-returned `blocked` to **`unclear`** before covering-test substitution and final normalization. Clear blocked metadata and covering-test requests for that row, retain sanitized original evidence, and add factory feedback explaining that browser verification was available but not completed. Recompute the overall result.\n\n   Converted rows enter the existing bounded feedback-round path. They do not trigger a new browser-specific retry or the environment-only retry by themselves. Preserve ordinary handling of non-browser checks.\n\n4. **Engine-owned infrastructure outcomes.** Determine preview startup failure only from the engine’s current preview setup/build/seed/serve/readiness execution. Preserve the pipeline failure and diagnostic without accepting a model’s claim as evidence. A prior round’s successful preview stage is not evidence that a current preview exists.\n\n   A ready preview with `browser_unavailable`, a remote invocation, or an unsupported browser platform is a separate browser infrastructure limitation. Retain the existing bounded environment-block handling; do not classify it as preview startup failure or a product defect.\n\n5. **No preview and resume.** Without a preview, omit the Browser section and preserve existing verification behavior. Persist enough attempt context to normalize pending results consistently after restart. Missing historical context means legacy/unknown, not failed startup.\n\n## Tests\nUse fakes only: no network, real browser, paid models or `gh`.\n- The generated prompt contains the actual preview URL, both exact viewport sizes and the instructions for observations at both. It is omitted without a preview.\n- Browser markers persist on criteria and holdouts. Records without them keep legacy behaviour, and the verifier can't change them.\n- With a ready preview and successful browser setup, a marked `blocked` row becomes `unclear`, produces feedback, fails overall, and reserves no environment retry for that row alone.\n- Browser unavailability, non-browser blocks, no preview, and the legacy and pending-result resume are each covered as distinct cases.\n- Reuse the existing preview-failure tests; add only the assertions this behaviour needs. Each focused test fails when its production change is removed.\n\n## Compatibility\nNew persisted fields are additive and tolerate absence. Historical runs need no browser markers. Existing trusted-base preview restoration and completed-work checkpoints are preserved.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Retaining, storing, serving or displaying screenshots (#1008).\n- Browser provisioning, confinement changes, new platforms or remote browser transport.\n- Image comparison and OCR.\n- New retries.\n- Broad UI-criterion guidance beyond the browser marker.\n\n## Budget\nHard stop: 180 changed source lines (additions plus deletions). Tests are reported separately.\n"
}
</github-data-json>
